-From e20bd175214b907167f41407889686aeb348d62c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
-From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
-Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 12:01:20 +0000
-Subject: [PATCH] Give lazy-loaded video a stable box, so it stops jumping on
- scroll
-MIME-Version: 1.0
-Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
-Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit
-
-The video-loop block never set a width or height — deliberate, since
-the media can be any shape — so with no metadata loaded yet, the
-element collapsed to the browser's placeholder size (300×150) and
-snapped to its real size only once the source finished loading. Combined
-with the lazy-load strategy that drops a video's source when it scrolls
-out of range, that collapse-then-snap happened every time a video came
-back into range, which is what read as the page lurching up and down.
-
-Learn the video's aspect ratio once, the first time its metadata has
-ever loaded, and hold it in state for good — unlike the source and
-`preload`, it does not get lost again when the video scrolls back out
-of range. Applied as an inline `aspect-ratio`, it reserves the video's
-real box before a single frame decodes, so the layout has nothing left
-to jump.
-
-Also: a video that's been active at least once keeps its `<source>`
-mounted permanently now, only downgrading `preload` to `metadata`
-(not `none`) while off-screen — a metadata-only fetch is a handful of
-bytes, and it's what lets the aspect ratio be re-learned instantly,
-from cache, every time a video re-enters the loading margin.
-
-Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
